{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00352 - MONDO_15166",
  "verbalization": "Tioguanine appears to be linked to Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a series of indications that ultimately converge on the AML subtype with the T(8;21)(q22;q22) translocation. The evidence suggests that Tioguanine is associated with \"Unclassified Acute Myeloid Leukemia,\" which is a broad category that includes various subtypes, including the T(8;21) translocation. This connection is further supported by the presence of Glasdegib and Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin as indications for AML, both of which are known to be used in the treatment of AML with this specific translocation. The overlapping pathways indicate that Tioguanine may act through a mechanism that is broadly applicable to AML, potentially influencing pathways that are involved in the development or progression of this disease. The integration of these indications suggests that Tioguanine may have a role in the treatment or management of AML with the T(8;21) translocation, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
